Behaviours:
- Add the project-filter group below the machine row, or below Search when that row is absent. Show it for one or more open groups, omit it for zero, and follow Search's collapsed visibility on web, desktop and mobile browsers.
- Keep only Search, Add project and New thread in the search bar. The project row and searchable +N popup edit the same sidebarProjectScopeKeys set as the Filter by project action; remove the duplicate header combobox and its unused helpers. Keep F6 in its sidebar region and let Tab follow the visible control order.
- Show machine-scoped project favicons in pinned-first order, then latest thread activity, falling back to project update time, then display name. The activity projection is the only place that order is computed; the row renders it as is, and the projection's equality reports any change in order or thread counts, so a broken tie reorders the row while unrelated timestamp ticks do not rerender it. Icons carry machine labels, loaded counts, state and interaction hints. Read quiet client metadata without added websocket traffic. Several-machine candidates require a member on any selected machine while retaining All-machines metadata.
- Fit icons with ResizeObserver, keeping the strip empty before measurement; retain selected icons up to capacity and put excess selections in searchable +N with selected count/highlight. Reserve clear-button space only while selected; never scroll or wrap. Freeze order and fitting during hover/open menus, clip rather than overlap +N, and recompute afterward.
- Plain click, Enter and Space replace only the visible part of the active list with one project, or clear that visible part when its only selected project is picked again. Preserve entries unavailable on the selected machines; All machines applies the same rule across every available project. Cmd-click on macOS, Ctrl-click elsewhere, and the matching modifier+Enter toggle a project without changing other selections. Apply the same rule to searchable +N picks and retain the current Show only / Hide mode. Canonicalize known aliases before selection changes; plain and additive picks retain unknown and absent keys while effective filtering ignores them. Right-click, ContextMenu and Shift+F10 open native project settings without consuming the next activation; preserve macOS Ctrl-click context menus, ignore non-primary clicks and announce hidden icons as hidden. Use the platform-aware one-line hint: Click: only this · ⌘-click: add or remove · Right-click: settings, with Ctrl off macOS.
- Keep Show only and Hide lists with one global mode. Explicit mode changes restore the destination list as stored when it has any project visible on the selected machines; otherwise retain its invisible entries and append only the visible source selection. Preserve the source list in either direction and recognize stored aliases when checking visibility. In Show only, the thread menu's Filter by project applies the visible-part plain-click rule; show Show all projects only for the sole visibly selected project and clear only the visible part. From Hide, Filter by project switches to Show only using its persisted entries unavailable on the selected machines plus the chosen project. Preserve the entire Hide list for switching back, with separate lists across reloads; only the active mode affects visibility.
- Persist one project filter independently of machine selection. Changing machines updates only the machine list; retain project entries for offline, removed and currently unselected machines. Ignore unavailable entries in Hide mode; when no Show only entry is available, show all threads on the selected machines. Migrate the persisted mode's nonempty sanitized top-level list first, then the active machine scope's nonempty list for that mode, then All machines, otherwise empty. Read legacy scope maps and singleton fields tolerantly and ignore extra fields. Persist both shared lists, mode and the upstream single-project compatibility field for older clients; retain sanitized inactive top-level lists during migration. Explicit machine clearing removes obsolete saved machine IDs even when the effective scope is already All.
- Compose machine/include/exclude scope through one predicate for thread partitions, folders, drafts/counts, search, keyboard/jump order, settled counts/tail/pagination and mode-specific empty states.
- Opening a hidden thread in an open project reveals only its project in the shared filter; preserve machine reveal, canonicalize legacy aliases and preserve store identity when nothing changes. Machine reveal uses the shared project lists and preserves the inactive list while rendering only the active mode.
- Provide x and counted Clear selection that clear only the visible part of the active list, preserving entries unavailable on the selected machines; All machines clears selections across available machines. Provide Show only/Hide radios, Hide indicator and Group threads by project through the existing Labs setting. Feature-owned styles respect reduced motion. Document filtering, mode restoration and carry, the shared filter across machines, inactive selections, reveal, settings shortcuts and grouping in the existing user guide.
- Project icons and every +N popup project show static top-right thread badges, including crossed Hide selections. Count Approval, Input, Request, Failed, Limited, Woke and unread Done threads, displaying 1-9 or 9+; use the most urgent state's row color (Approval/Input/Request, Failed, Limited, Woke, Done). Only Working shows a static dot; no matching state shows nothing. +N sums only the projects it hides. Tooltip and accessible names include per-state counts.
- Explain the badges in a Badge legend popover, opened from the project row's ⋯ menu or the command palette's Show project badge legend action. It lists the states in priority order with the live badge swatches, the tooltip's state names (Needs input) and one-line meanings, then the colour rule, a pointer to hover an icon for exact counts (three states share the amber colour), the number (up to 9+, Working excluded), dot, icon order (selected projects stay in the row) and +N (projects that do not fit) rules. The popover anchors to the ⋯ button, closes with Escape or an outside click and returns focus there. The palette action appears only while a project row renders at least one project, opens the legend after the palette has restored focus, and expands a collapsed desktop sidebar first; the legend closes when the row empties and never opens later from a request made while nothing rendered. Row tooltips stay off while the legend is open.
- Share pure top-status derivation, Woke/unread precedence and color tokens with SidebarThreadRow. Retain one badge aggregate atom per resolved machine set in a module-level atom family, with stable project inputs and structural equality for badge-relevant thread inputs and aggregate results. Ignore unrelated renames; subscribe in the connected project row so badge changes do not subscribe or rerender the main Sidebar. Exclude nested subagents/delegated threads through the row visibility predicate; share the config-loaded settled/snoozed predicate with row partitions, and exclude archived/deleted threads and machines outside scope. Observe local visit fallback and precise snooze deadlines without added per-thread subscriptions or websocket traffic; preserve ResizeObserver behavior and avoid badge animation.

Icon order, tooltip and mode switch (these replace the icon order, the Badge legend popover and the ⋯ menu described above):
- Order project icons by a per-device preference with three values. Name (the default) puts pinned projects first, then display name, then project key; thread timestamps never influence it, so a thread update neither reorders nor re-renders the row while thread counts still update. Latest activity keeps the previous order: pinned, latest thread activity falling back to project update time, display name, project key. Manual puts pinned projects first, then the stored manual project order (the same uiStateStore.projectOrder that the upstream Project order → Manual setting reads), then projects not in that order by display name. The +N popup lists projects in the same order and the fit rule is unchanged.
- Persist the icon order in the UI state store next to the project filter, restore it with the rest of that store and sanitize unknown values to Name. No contract changes.
- Rank a project in the manual order by its logical group on every machine: sidebarProjectManualRanks maps each member's physical project key to the earliest stored position of any key of the unscoped group (its members' physical keys and legacy cwd keys). The icon row and the grouped folder headers sort through one shared function, sortSidebarProjectGroups, with these ranks, so narrowing the view to one machine or to several keeps the manual order. The stored order is read only while Manual is selected, so the other orders never react to it.
- Show Project icon order in Settings → Labs directly below Boring sidebar, with a Name (A–Z) / Latest activity / Manual select, a reset button while it is not Name, and a settings search entry. While Manual is selected, show a sortable list directly under the row: every open project group from all machines in the effective order, each with a drag handle named "Reorder <project>, position <n>", the project favicon, the name and a quiet pin marker for pinned projects. The list is not rendered for the other orders.
- A drop in that list, by mouse, touch or keyboard (Space, arrow keys, Space), moves exactly the dragged project through reorderProjects. Every key that resolves to it (its members' physical keys and the legacy cwd keys that match only it) moves together next to the target project's ranking key; every other stored key keeps its relative order, including pinned projects, projects on offline machines and legacy keys that match no listed project. A legacy cwd key that matches more than one listed group is first replaced at its position by the physical keys of every group it matched, in display order, and is never a drag or target key. Listed projects without a stored key are appended after the stored order by name before a move. Pinned-first applies only when sorting for display; a drop across the pinned boundary stays within the dragged project's section.
- Lay out the project icon tooltip as sections: the name with a Selected, Shown or Hidden chip; a muted line with the machine icon and names (only when the row shows machines) and the thread count; the non-zero badge states in priority order as coloured dot, count and label; the click, ⌘/Ctrl click and right-click shortcuts as a keycap grid; and the badge legend with the eight live swatches and the colour, number and dot rule. The +N tooltip shows the hidden-project count, the selected count when any, and the same coloured counts line. Accessible names keep their per-state counts. Tooltip content is a component that renders only while its tooltip is open.
- Expose per-state badge entries (state, count, label, colour) from the shared badge summary; the description string is built from them.
- Remove the separate badge legend: its popover, meanings table and request listeners, the legend-request module, the command palette action Show project badge legend, and the legend-only styles.
- Replace the ⋯ menu with one 28 px mode switch. Its face is an eye for Show selected and a destructively tinted crossed eye for Hide selected; a click switches between them. While ⌘ (Ctrl off macOS) is held anywhere in the app the face is a group icon, pressed with the primary tint while grouping is on, and a click toggles Group threads by project through the boringSidebarEnabled client setting. While the left ⌥ (left Alt) is held the face is a gear and a click opens Settings → Labs at the Boring sidebar row. The selection modifier wins when both are held, a window blur or a paste resets the face, and only the switch re-renders when a modifier changes. The click action comes from the click event's own modifier flags; a settings click also needs the tracked left Alt. Enter and Space switch the mode, modifier+Enter toggles grouping and left Alt+Enter opens the settings.
- With only the right Alt held the switch keeps its plain face and a click or Enter does the plain action. Outside macOS the switch ignores AltGr (the AltGraph key state): it shows neither the group nor the gear face and does the plain action. On macOS, where Firefox reports AltGraph for both Option keys, the left Option key still shows the gear face.
- The switch tooltip lists the three actions with keycaps (Click, ⌘/Ctrl Click, Left ⌥/Left Alt Click) and its accessible name states the current face, with aria-pressed on the group face.
- Open a menu with Show selected / Hide selected, Group threads by project and Sidebar settings from the switch on right-click, the ContextMenu key, Shift+F10 and a touch long-press (which swallows the following click), through the shared native or DOM context menu. The row freezes and row tooltips stay off while it is open. Clear selection remains the × button.
- Update the user guide: icon order with its setting and the Manual list, the switch with its modifier actions and menu, the left-Alt rule, and the legend in the icon tooltip.

Integration details:
- Preserve upstream pin-first project order, overflow pin controls and keyboard pinning, archived-project omission, pinned empty folders, and scroll resets for every explicit filter mutation. Preserve side-chat/subagent badge exclusions and popup positioning/focus behavior. Opening an archived-project thread clears visible Show-only keys when necessary to reveal it, while retaining dormant keys and the inactive list.
- The persisted-set additive toggle also changes the native header Shift-pick behavior versus upstream: upstream toggled resolved keys and dropped missing ones; this feature toggles persisted keys and preserves missing/absent selections. The final project-row UI replaces that native header picker rather than retaining a second picker.
- The project row replaces upstream's header project combobox (#29639), so the unused apps/web/src/components/ProjectComboboxRow.tsx is deleted.
- Preparing threads (upstream Setting up, #28832) get a preparing entry in the shared top-status table in Sidebar.logic.ts (Setting up, working icon, info colour), and project badges count them as working.
- The badge legend and the per-state counts are reachable by hover and keyboard focus only; touch devices have no way to open them.
- When a modifier is released while a native context menu holds the keyboard, the switch keeps the modifier face until the next key event; the click still follows the click's own modifier flags.
- legacyProjectCwdPreferenceKey lives in sidebarProjectRow.ts and uiStateStore re-exports it, because uiStateStore already imports that module.

Port to 965ddc1d6c:
- Upstream #33131 removed the legacy sidebar setting (legacySidebarEnabled) and the useBoringSidebarEnabled hook. The project row's Group threads by project item reads boringSidebarEnabled through useClientSettings and writes only boringSidebarEnabled, like upstream's Labs toggle; the project-row browser test mocks useClientSettings instead of the removed hook.

